//! flags: -p ssa -b llvm

// KNOWN LIMITATION -- this golden records a bug, not the behaviour we want.
// When the bug is fixed this test will fail; re-bless it with the IR it then
// produces and move it out of "known-limitation".
//
// A `mut` array local with an initializer is miscompiled through the SSA
// pipeline. The declaration allocates storage for four integers, and then the
// initializer builds a second array and stores a *handle* to it into the
// first, so the array begins with a pointer where its first two elements
// should be and the two after that are never written at all.
//
// The golden below says so outright: the first two values printed are
// `ptrtoint` of the array's own address, split into halves, and the other two
// are `undef`. It should be printing 5, 6, 7, 8, which is what the same
// program compiled without `-p ssa` does.
//
// It is diffed as IR rather than run because what it prints is an address and
// uninitialized memory, neither of which is the same twice. Nothing here
// involves parfor, vectorization or loops; it is the SSA lowering of the
// declaration itself.
func main() -> i32 {
    a : mut array[i32, 4] = {5, 6, 7, 8};
    print(a[0], a[1], a[2], a[3]);
    return 0;
}
